  {
    "id": "mit-5-2-definition-antiderivative",
    "section_id": "mit-5-2-antiderivatives",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "definition",
    "heading": "Antiderivatives",
    "order": 10,
    "pdf_page": 6,
    "printed_page": 234,
    "text": "An antiderivative of f is a function whose derivative is f. Any two antiderivatives differ by a constant, so the general antiderivative includes an added constant C.",
    "formulas": [
      "F'(x) = f(x)",
      "general antiderivative = F(x) + C"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "antiderivative",
      "constant of integration"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-2-rule-power-antiderivative",
    "section_id": "mit-5-2-antiderivatives",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "rule",
    "heading": "Antiderivative of a power",
    "order": 20,
    "pdf_page": 7,
    "printed_page": 235,
    "text": "Reversing the power rule, the antiderivative of x to the n is x to the n plus one over n plus one, valid whenever n is not minus one.",
    "formulas": [
      "integral x^n dx = x^(n+1)/(n+1) + C",
      "valid for n != -1"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "antiderivative",
      "power rule reversed"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-2-example-antiderivative",
    "section_id": "mit-5-2-antiderivatives",
    "content_type": "example",
    "subtype": "worked_example",
    "heading": "Finding an antiderivative",
    "order": 30,
    "pdf_page": 8,
    "printed_page": 236,
    "text": "The antiderivative of 3x^2 + 2 is x^3 + 2x plus a constant, because differentiating that returns the original function.",
    "formulas": [
      "integral (3x^2 + 2) dx = x^3 + 2x + C"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "antiderivative",
      "term by term"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-3-concept-summation-integration",
    "section_id": "mit-5-3-summation-versus-integration",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "concept_explanation",
    "heading": "Summation versus integration",
    "order": 10,
    "pdf_page": 12,
    "printed_page": 240,
    "text": "Sums add discrete terms while integrals add continuous pieces. A finite sum with many small terms approaches the integral, so integration is the continuous limit of summation.",
    "formulas": [
      "sum of f(i) Delta x -> integral of f dx",
      "discrete sum vs continuous integral"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "summation",
      "integration",
      "continuous limit"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-3-rule-sum-formulas",
    "section_id": "mit-5-3-summation-versus-integration",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "rule",
    "heading": "Closed forms for sums",
    "order": 20,
    "pdf_page": 13,
    "printed_page": 241,
    "text": "The sum of the first n integers and of the first n squares have closed forms. Dividing by powers of n and letting n grow recovers the corresponding integrals.",
    "formulas": [
      "sum_(1)^(n) i = n(n+1)/2",
      "sum_(1)^(n) i^2 = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "sum of integers",
      "sum of squares",
      "closed form"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-3-example-area-by-sums",
    "section_id": "mit-5-3-summation-versus-integration",
    "content_type": "example",
