  {
    "id": "mit-5-4-rule-substitution",
    "section_id": "mit-5-4-indefinite-integrals-and-substitutions",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "rule",
    "heading": "Integration by substitution",
    "order": 10,
    "pdf_page": 21,
    "printed_page": 249,
    "text": "Substitution reverses the chain rule: replacing an inner function by u and dx by du over the inner derivative turns a hard integral into a simple one.",
    "formulas": [
      "integral f(g(x)) g'(x) dx = integral f(u) du",
      "u = g(x), du = g'(x) dx"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "substitution",
      "chain rule reversed",
      "indefinite integral"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-4-example-substitution",
    "section_id": "mit-5-4-indefinite-integrals-and-substitutions",
    "content_type": "example",
    "subtype": "worked_example",
    "heading": "A substitution example",
    "order": 20,
    "pdf_page": 22,
    "printed_page": 250,
    "text": "For the integral of 2x times cos(x^2), let u = x^2 so du = 2x dx. The integral becomes the integral of cos u, which is sin u, giving sin(x^2) plus a constant.",
    "formulas": [
      "u = x^2, du = 2x dx",
      "integral 2x cos(x^2) dx = sin(x^2) + C"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "substitution",
      "trigonometric integral"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-5-definition-definite-integral",
    "section_id": "mit-5-5-the-definite-integral",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "definition",
    "heading": "The definite integral",
    "order": 10,
    "pdf_page": 26,
    "printed_page": 254,
    "text": "The definite integral is the limit of Riemann sums over an interval and equals the signed area between the graph and the axis. Area below the axis counts as negative.",
    "formulas": [
      "integral_a^b f(x) dx = lim sum f(x_i) Delta x",
      "signed area between curve and axis"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "definite integral",
      "Riemann sum",
      "signed area"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-5-rule-limits-of-integration",
    "section_id": "mit-5-5-the-definite-integral",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "rule",
    "heading": "Limits and orientation",
    "order": 20,
    "pdf_page": 27,
    "printed_page": 255,
    "text": "Swapping the limits of integration changes the sign, and an integral over a zero-width interval is zero. Splitting the interval adds the integrals over the parts.",
    "formulas": [
      "integral_a^b = - integral_b^a",
      "integral_a^a = 0",
      "integral_a^c = integral_a^b + integral_b^c"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "orientation",
      "additivity",
      "limits of integration"
    ]
  },
  {
