  {
    "id": "mit-5-7-rule-fundamental-theorem",
    "section_id": "mit-5-7-the-fundamental-theorem-and-its-applications",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "rule",
    "heading": "The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus",
    "order": 10,
    "pdf_page": 39,
    "printed_page": 267,
    "text": "The Fundamental Theorem links derivatives and integrals: a definite integral equals the change in an antiderivative, and differentiating an integral with variable upper limit returns the integrand.",
    "formulas": [
      "integral_a^b f(x) dx = F(b) - F(a)",
      "d/dx integral_a^x f(t) dt = f(x)"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "fundamental theorem",
      "antiderivative",
      "evaluation"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "mit-5-7-concept-two-parts",
    "section_id": "mit-5-7-the-fundamental-theorem-and-its-applications",
    "content_type": "concept",
    "subtype": "concept_explanation",
    "heading": "Why the two parts fit together",
    "order": 20,
    "pdf_page": 40,
    "printed_page": 268,
    "text": "Accumulating a rate and then taking its derivative are inverse operations. This is why finding areas reduces to finding antiderivatives instead of computing limits of sums.",
    "formulas": [
      "differentiation undoes integration",
      "integration undoes differentiation"
    ],
    "figure_ids": [],
    "knowledge_points": [
      "inverse operations",
      "accumulation",
      "rate"
    ]
  },
  {
